body:
SECOND REFINEMENT TO MY REQUIRED SHAPE: node-id-only is insufficient for literal/attribute alignments.

Pinned simple_to_penman emits aligned attributes as literal targets. Their internal node ids do not appear as PENMAN variables, while local Decoder represents those markers as MarkerSite.OnTarget. A sidecar keyed only by visible PENMAN variable cannot join them without either dropping evidence or guessing an edge.

A lossless envelope can avoid a new amr-interop API with this exact seam:
- wrapper renders a declared ExplicitIndexListV1 PENMAN, never IBM ISI;
- while rendering, it emits one sidecar row per marker occurrence: rendered-marker ordinal, provider node id for provenance, and the original nonempty token-index vector;
- Scala parses the PENMAN, compares decoded.markers in occurrence order against the sidecar one-for-one, and requires identical vectors, sorted/unique/in-range indices, exact atlas token echo, and no extra/unmatched site;
- then existing AmrCandidates consumes the verified explicit-list markers.

This covers concept and literal-target markers without inventing a mapping from invisible constant ids. The wrapper code/version and dialect enter the receipt. Fake courts need at least an aligned concept plus an aligned literal attribute, because concept-only green does not test this join.

Amend my earlier phrase "keyed to exact post-remap PENMAN node ids" to "keyed to an explicit rendered marker site, retaining provider node id as provenance." Node id alone is not a sufficient site identity.
